1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,740
Welcome to Episode Six, subtopic seven point four: Daily Bias.

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:13,984
So far we have studied how price may be delivered and when we watch it. Daily Bias asks which direction we expect, and why.

3
00:00:14,404 --> 00:00:20,908
You will learn to build bias from structure, liquidity and location, and to write down what would prove it wrong.

4
00:00:22,728 --> 00:00:26,688
Daily Bias is a directional working hypothesis for the day.

5
00:00:27,108 --> 00:00:34,956
A bullish bias expects the day to favour higher prices and buy-side objectives. A bearish bias expects the opposite.

6
00:00:35,376 --> 00:00:40,848
A neutral bias means there is no clear directional edge. That is a legitimate answer.

7
00:00:41,268 --> 00:00:47,700
We set bias before intraday analysis because it frames which setups we study, and which we ignore.

8
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:54,936
But bias is not a guaranteed prediction. It is a hypothesis that should be updated when conditions change.

9
00:00:55,356 --> 00:01:03,300
And bias is not confirmation. A directional expectation still needs lower-timeframe evidence before any trade decision.

10
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:13,256
Step one is higher-timeframe market structure, from Level Two. This is a daily chart. First, identify the broader trend.

11
00:01:13,676 --> 00:01:18,572
Next, label the meaningful swing points. Higher highs and higher lows.

12
00:01:18,992 --> 00:01:24,128
Evaluate the latest structural breaks. Two breaks of structure to the upside.

13
00:01:24,548 --> 00:01:29,132
The external structure is a sequence of higher highs and higher lows.

14
00:01:29,552 --> 00:01:37,064
Then ask: is price trending, retracing or consolidating? Right now, it is retracing from the last high.

15
00:01:37,484 --> 00:01:43,292
So the initial hypothesis is bullish, but we have not yet checked liquidity or location.

16
00:01:45,112 --> 00:01:52,264
Step two is the Draw on Liquidity: the liquidity pool price is judged most likely to move toward next.

17
00:01:52,684 --> 00:02:00,124
Above, buy-side liquidity rests beyond the old high. This is external liquidity, at the edge of the range.

18
00:02:00,544 --> 00:02:05,968
Below, sell-side liquidity rests beneath the last swing low. Also external.

19
00:02:06,388 --> 00:02:13,372
Inside the range, the fair value gap is internal liquidity, a place price may revisit along the way.

20
00:02:13,792 --> 00:02:20,728
With bullish structure and price retracing, a reasonable draw is the buy-side liquidity above the old high.

21
00:02:21,148 --> 00:02:27,004
But a Draw on Liquidity is an analytical expectation, not a guaranteed destination.

22
00:02:28,824 --> 00:02:35,304
Which levels are candidates for the draw? The previous day high and low are the most common intraday objectives.

23
00:02:35,724 --> 00:02:40,620
The previous week high and low are larger objectives that may take several days.

24
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:45,432
Equal highs and equal lows, but only where they are genuinely equal.

25
00:02:45,852 --> 00:02:51,036
Old swing highs and lows at the edges of the dealing range are external liquidity.

26
00:02:51,456 --> 00:02:59,208
And imbalances inside the range are internal liquidity, which price may rebalance before reaching the external target.

27
00:03:01,028 --> 00:03:08,852
Step three is location, from Level Six. Define the dealing range from the latest significant swing low to swing high.

28
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:15,008
Equilibrium is the fifty percent level. Below it is discount; above it is premium.

29
00:03:15,428 --> 00:03:23,276
The optimal trade entry zone sits between the sixty-two and seventy-nine percent retracement. Price is trading inside it now.

30
00:03:23,696 --> 00:03:28,424
Just below price is a bullish fair value gap, a bullish PD array.

31
00:03:28,844 --> 00:03:35,996
Bullish structure with price in discount, approaching a bullish PD array, supports the bullish hypothesis.

32
00:03:36,416 --> 00:03:47,912
If the same structure were trading in premium, into a bearish array, the bullish case would be weaker. And if the daily and intraday locations conflict, confidence drops further.

33
00:03:49,732 --> 00:03:53,020
On an hourly chart, mark yesterday’s levels.

34
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:57,040
The previous day high and low are liquidity references.

35
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:03,364
The previous day open and close add context: did yesterday close strong or weak?

36
00:04:03,784 --> 00:04:08,152
The previous week high and low play the same role on the weekly scale.

37
00:04:08,572 --> 00:04:09,868
Today begins.

38
00:04:10,288 --> 00:04:14,296
Early in the day, price trades below the previous day low.

39
00:04:14,716 --> 00:04:20,164
Then it reverses and runs toward the previous day high, consistent with the bullish draw.

40
00:04:20,584 --> 00:04:26,320
But do not assume every previous high or low will be targeted. Many days take neither.

41
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:34,020
Let’s assemble a bullish Daily Bias model. One: higher-timeframe bullish structure.

42
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:41,640
Two and three: relevant external liquidity above, giving a potential draw toward buy-side liquidity.

43
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:44,772
Four: price trading into discount.

44
00:04:45,192 --> 00:04:49,176
Five: a bullish PD array, the fair value gap.

45
00:04:49,596 --> 00:04:56,652
Six: on the fifteen-minute chart, a possible liquidity interaction. Sell-side liquidity is swept.

46
00:04:57,072 --> 00:05:02,016
Seven: lower-timeframe bullish displacement and a market structure shift.

47
00:05:02,436 --> 00:05:08,628
Eight: together, these give a potential bullish intraday scenario. Potential, not certain.

48
00:05:10,448 --> 00:05:14,768
Now the alternative. The sell-side sweep happens, just as before.

49
00:05:15,188 --> 00:05:21,140
But there is no reclaim, no displacement, and no structure shift. Price keeps falling.

50
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:29,768
It breaks the invalidation level you wrote down before the session, for example the failure of the discount array or the higher-timeframe low.

51
00:05:30,188 --> 00:05:37,892
The bullish bias is invalid. Update it to neutral, or re-assess for bearish conditions. Do not defend it.

52
00:05:39,712 --> 00:05:47,032
The bearish model is the inverse. One: higher-timeframe bearish structure, lower highs and lower lows.

53
00:05:47,452 --> 00:05:53,572
Two and three: external sell-side liquidity below, giving a potential draw lower.

54
00:05:53,992 --> 00:05:56,824
Four: price retracing into premium.

55
00:05:57,244 --> 00:06:01,660
Five: a bearish PD array, a bearish fair value gap.

56
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:06,616
Six: on the lower timeframe, buy-side liquidity is swept.

57
00:06:07,036 --> 00:06:11,020
Seven: bearish displacement and a market structure shift.

58
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:21,928
Eight: a potential bearish intraday scenario. The alternative, where price reclaims and breaks the invalidation above, would end the bearish bias in the same way.

59
00:06:23,748 --> 00:06:32,052
Sometimes the market offers no clear direction. Here, higher-timeframe structure is conflicting: a range after a sharp decline.

60
00:06:32,472 --> 00:06:39,720
Price is trapped between buy-side liquidity above the equal highs and sell-side liquidity below the equal lows.

61
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:41,988
It sits near equilibrium.

62
00:06:42,408 --> 00:06:45,792
And there is no clear displacement in either direction.

63
00:06:46,212 --> 00:06:53,508
Add major event risk, or overlapping and conflicting PD arrays, and the picture is even less clear.

64
00:06:53,928 --> 00:07:01,584
In these conditions, neutral is a valid analytical conclusion. Not having an opinion is sometimes the professional opinion.

65
00:07:03,404 --> 00:07:14,372
Here is the CMI Daily Bias checklist. One: what is the higher-timeframe structure? Two: what is the latest meaningful break of structure or change of character?

66
00:07:14,792 --> 00:07:21,680
Three and four: where is price in the dealing range, and is it in premium, discount, or near equilibrium?

67
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:28,124
Five and six: which liquidity pools are relevant, and what is the potential draw on liquidity?

68
00:07:28,544 --> 00:07:33,584
Seven and eight: are there relevant PD arrays, and is there displacement?

69
00:07:34,004 --> 00:07:39,116
Nine: what would invalidate the current bias? Write this down before the session.

70
00:07:39,536 --> 00:07:43,712
And ten: is the bias bullish, bearish or neutral?

71
00:07:45,532 --> 00:07:50,500
Common mistakes. Assuming bullish structure guarantees a bullish day.

72
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:53,032
Using only one timeframe.

73
00:07:53,452 --> 00:07:55,732
Ignoring external liquidity.

74
00:07:56,152 --> 00:07:58,840
Changing bias after every candle.

75
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:02,212
Treating Daily Bias as a fixed prediction.

76
00:08:02,632 --> 00:08:05,392
Ignoring scheduled economic events.

77
00:08:05,812 --> 00:08:08,668
Forcing a bullish or bearish conclusion.

78
00:08:09,088 --> 00:08:11,752
And ignoring invalidation conditions.

79
00:08:13,572 --> 00:08:18,804
To summarise. Daily Bias is a working hypothesis, not a forecast.

80
00:08:19,224 --> 00:08:27,768
Build it from structure, liquidity, location and PD arrays. The draw on liquidity frames the day, but is never guaranteed.

81
00:08:28,188 --> 00:08:33,468
Bias still needs lower-timeframe confirmation, and neutral is a valid answer.

82
00:08:33,888 --> 00:08:37,944
Above all, write your invalidation before the session begins.

83
00:08:39,764 --> 00:08:45,500
Time to check your understanding. Pause the video and answer these five questions in your own words.

84
00:08:47,420 --> 00:08:52,844
Write down your reasoning, not just a one-word answer. The reasoning is what we assess.

85
00:08:56,664 --> 00:09:02,880
Here are the short answers. Compare them with your reasoning; the workbook explains each one in full.

86
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:15,796
Next, two chart exercises. Use your own charting platform and mark the points described.

87
00:09:16,216 --> 00:09:21,400
Then complete the practical task, and record what would make your interpretation invalid.

88
